Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of it at x = 0, through order 4:
  it = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24 + O(x^5)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 1 1 1/2 1/6 1/24
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 8.47e-08 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 2.63e-09 at ± 1/20, 8.17e-11 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^5.0; expected >= 5)
> taylor expansion of log x about 1 to order 3
  note: "log" without a base: read as the natural log, ln, not log base 2 or log base 10 (say "natural log", "log base 2" or "log base 10", or "assume log means base 2")
  ucas: it(x) = log(x)
  ucas: taylor it at 1 order 3
Taylor series of it at x = 1, through order 3:
  it = x - 1 - (x - 1)^2/2 + (x - 1)^3/3 + O((x - 1)^4)
  as x -> 1; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(1)/k!: 0 1 -1/2 1/3
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 2.72e-05 at x = 1 ± 1/10, 1.63e-06 at ± 1/20, 9.97e-08 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^4.0; expected >= 4)
> dy/dx for x^2 + y^2 = 1
  ucas: implicit x^2 + y^2 = 1 for y wrt x
on the curve x^2 + y^2 - 1 = 0, y as a function of x:
  dy/dx = -F_x/F_y = -x/y
  holds where F_y = 2y != 0 (the implicit function theorem: there y is locally a differentiable function of x)
  check: matches the slope of the curve (points solved by bisection and Newton) at 12 points (max relative difference 2e-12)
> differentiate x^2 + y^2 = 25 implicitly
  ucas: implicit x^2 + y^2 = 25
on the curve x^2 + y^2 - 25 = 0, y as a function of x:
  dy/dx = -F_x/F_y = -x/y
  holds where F_y = 2y != 0 (the implicit function theorem: there y is locally a differentiable function of x)
  check: matches the slope of the curve (points solved by bisection and Newton) at 12 points (max relative difference 8e-12)
> antiderivative of 1/(1 + x^2)
  ucas: it(x) = 1/(1 + x^2)
  ucas: antiderivative it
∫ 1/(x^2 + 1) dx
  = atan(x) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 0)
> anti-derivative of x e^x
  ucas: it(x) = x*e^x
  ucas: antiderivative it
∫ x*e^x dx
  = x*exp(x) - exp(x) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 1.5e-10)
> primitive of cos(x)^2
  ucas: it(x) = cos(x)^2
  ucas: antiderivative it
∫ cos(x)^2 dx
  = x/2 + sin(2x)/4 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 2.2e-16)
> indefinite integral of x log x
  ucas: it(x) = x*log(x)
  ucas: antiderivative it
∫ x*log(x) dx
  = x^2*log(x)/2 - x^2/4 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 200 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 3.6e-15)
> the antiderivative of x^2 from 0 to 1
  note: an antiderivative is indefinite; with bounds this is the definite integral
  ucas: it(x) = x^2
  ucas: integrate it from 0 to 1
∫ x^2 dx
  = x^3/3 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 1] (max difference 1.1e-16)
  from 0 to 1: F(1) - F(0) = 0.333333333333333
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 0.333333333333333, estimated error 3.7e-15
  the two agree to 0
